{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_654183346#13_1186396961","relevance":3,"reason":"This NHS inform passage gives direct UK evidence on ethnic drivers of type 2 diabetes: it states type 2 diabetes is up to six times more common in South Asian communities and three times more common among African/African-Caribbean people in the UK, with complications at a younger age. This directly supports why Britain may have more under-40 diabetes in populations at higher and earlier risk.","learned":"In the UK, type 2 diabetes is much more common among South Asian and African/African-Caribbean communities, and these groups face younger-age risks and complications."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"type 2 diabetes\" (+ deprivation deprived poverty \"poorest\") (+ \"young people\" \"young adults\" \"under 40\" \"children\") (+ uk britain england))","reason":"Search for UK evidence on socioeconomic deprivation as another likely driver of rising young-onset type 2 diabetes, complementing the strong obesity and ethnicity evidence already found."}}
