{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_433809561#0_801573553","relevance":1,"reason":"This passage gives broad claims about pre-diabetes rising among British adults and mentions diabetes incidence in children, but it is not focused on Britain’s under-40 type 2 diabetes trend, does not provide a clear cause, and the deprivation match is incidental.","learned":"Broad pre-diabetes articles may mention a large rise among British adults, but this query did not retrieve direct deprivation evidence for young-onset diabetes."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"type 2 diabetes\" (+ deprivation deprived \"poorest areas\" poverty) (+ children adolescents \"young people\") (+ england uk) (+ obesity overweight))","reason":"Refocus on UK/England children or young people with type 2 diabetes and force deprivation/poverty plus obesity terms to find direct evidence on socioeconomic drivers of young-onset diabetes."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"type 2 diabetes\" (+ deprivation deprived \"poorest areas\" poverty) (+ children adolescents \"young people\") (+ england uk) (+ obesity overweight))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_608069640#6_1380670496","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q538","rank":3,"snippet":"obesity levels reaching 21.9 percent in youth living in households that made less than the federal-poverty level. Obesity rates for youth in households that made at least 400 percent of the federal <cover>poverty level were 9.4 percent. MORE: Mom's lifestyle could be key to childhood obesity, study suggests “These differences by race, ethnicity and geography did not happen by change, Besser said. “ They are a result of discriminatory policies and systems that have been in place for decades. However, we have the power to change these outcomes and make our nation a more equitable society. The more we understand the barriers to good health, the more we can do to address them.” Obesity early in life can put individuals at greater risk for diseases including type 2 diabetes, heart disease and certain types of cancer. One study in the New England Journal of Medicine found that five-year-olds who were overweight were four times more likely (than healthy weight children) </cover>to be overweight at age 14. Policy shifts in recent years are showing some progression in the right direction.\" , \"start_char\": 3724.000000 , \"title\":","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1480140766#12_3016024231","rank":1,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1480140766#13_3016026294","rank":2,"relevance":1}]}}
