{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q539"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_608069640#7_1380672131","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q539","rank":15,"snippet":"5479.000000 , \"headings\": \"Childhood obesity rate in Texas remained unchanged in 2018, according to report Childhood obesity rate in Texas remained unchanged in 2018, according to report\" , \"segment\": \"However, we have the power to change these outcomes and make our nation a more equitable society. The more we understand the barriers to good health, the more we can do to address them.” <cover>Obesity early in life can put individuals at greater risk for diseases including type 2 diabetes, heart disease and certain types of cancer. One study in the New England Journal of Medicine found that five-year-olds who were overweight were four times more likely (than healthy weight children) </cover>to be overweight at age 14. Policy shifts in recent years are showing some progression in the right direction. Updated nutrition standards for school lunches in 2010 with the Healthy, Hunger-Free Kids Act is helping more than 30 million children enjoy healthier school breakfasts, lunches and snacks. Obesity rates for children participating in the Women, Infants & Children (WIC) program show a decline from 15.9 percent in 2010 to 13.8 percent in 2016. State ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_608069640#6_1380670496","rank":14,"relevance":1}]}}
